-<h1>XR Gate: Windows installation with WSL2</h1>
-<h2>1. Prepare Windows</h2>
+<h1>XR Gate on Windows</h1>
+<h2>1. Download and prepare</h2>
 <ol start="1">
-<li>Use Windows 11 x64 with an AVX2-capable CPU.</li>
+<li>Use Windows 11 x64 and an AVX2-capable CPU.</li>
 <li>Install Steam and SteamVR. Open SteamVR once, then close it.</li>
-<li>Extract the entire archive to a permanent folder, such as C:\XR-Gate-WSL. Keep the linux and windows subfolders next to XrGate-Wsl.ps1.</li>
-<li>Open a regular Windows PowerShell window in that folder. Run the commands below in Windows.</li>
-</ol>
-<h2>2. Install XR Gate</h2>
-<ol start="1">
-<li>Double-click INSTALL.cmd, or run:</li>
-</ol>
-<pre><code>.\INSTALL.cmd</code></pre>
-<p>For Gear VR controllers, use this command instead:</p>
-<pre><code>.\INSTALL.cmd -GearVR</code></pre>
+<li>Download <strong>xr-gate-windows.zip</strong> from <a href="https://github.com/vladoshub/xr-gate/releases">Releases</a>, or the <strong>xr-gate-windows</strong> artifact from your CI run.</li>
+<li>Extract the ZIP to a permanent folder. Open its <code>xr-gate-windows</code> folder. Keep the <code>drivers</code>, <code>hardware</code>, <code>linux</code> and <code>windows</code> subfolders together.</li>
+<li>Open a regular Windows PowerShell in this folder. Run all commands below there.</li>
+</ol>
+<h2>2. Configure your hardware</h2>
+<p>For XREAL Air 2 Ultra, the supplied preset is ready to use. Create your settings file once:</p>
+<pre><code>if (!(Test-Path .env)) { Copy-Item .env.example .env }
+</code></pre>
+<p>For another device, copy the generic template:</p>
+<pre><code>Copy-Item hardware\generic.env.example hardware\my-device.env
+</code></pre>
+<p>Edit <code>hardware/my-device.env</code>, then set <code>XR_HARDWARE_ENV=hardware/my-device.env</code> in <code>.env</code>.</p>
+<ol start="1">
+<li>Set <code>XR_PROFILE</code> to the runtime profile and <code>XR_TARGET_DEVICE</code> to its device name.</li>
+<li>Set <code>XR_USB_IDS</code> to the camera/IMU VID:PID pairs. For a manual selection, run <code>usbipd list</code> and set <code>XR_USB_BUS_IDS</code> in <code>.env</code>. For multiple devices, separate the IDs with commas. <code>XR_USB_INSTANCE_IDS</code> accepts stable Windows USB instance paths instead.</li>
+<li>Set Linux camera, HID, serial and input paths when needed: <code>XR_CAMERA_DEVICE</code>, <code>XR_HIDRAW_DEVICES</code>, <code>XR_SERIAL_DEVICES</code>, <code>XR_INPUT_DEVICES</code>. Enable <code>XR_REQUIRE_CAMERA=1</code> if the profile uses a camera.</li>
+<li>Set <code>XR_DEVICE_ACCESS_SCRIPT</code> to the matching setup script inside the release, or leave it empty. List the required Linux modules in <code>XR_KERNEL_MODULES</code>.</li>
+<li>Set <code>XR_WINDOW_WIDTH</code>, <code>XR_WINDOW_HEIGHT</code>, <code>XR_RENDER_WIDTH</code>, <code>XR_RENDER_HEIGHT</code>, <code>XR_DISPLAY_FREQUENCY_HZ</code> and the optical settings for your display.</li>
+</ol>
+<p>To override saved choices, put the setting directly in <code>.env</code>. Select a frequency and <code>XR_DISPLAY_MODE</code> (<code>direct</code> or <code>extended_sbs</code>) included in the archive. For a dedicated Bluetooth dongle, set <code>XR_BLUETOOTH_BUS_ID</code> to its BUSID. Set <code>XR_BLUETOOTH=0</code> to disable a saved dongle, or <code>XR_GEARVR=0</code> to disable Gear VR. Set <code>XR_STEAMVR_PATH</code> if SteamVR is not found automatically.</p>
+<h2>3. Install</h2>
+<ol start="1">
+<li>Double-click <strong>INSTALL.cmd</strong>, or run:</li>
+</ol>
+<pre><code>.\INSTALL.cmd
+</code></pre>
+<p>For Gear VR controllers, use:</p>
+<pre><code>.\INSTALL.cmd -GearVR
+</code></pre>
 <ol start="2">
-<li>Approve the Windows administrator prompt.</li>
-<li>If a Windows restart is requested, save your work, restart Windows, and repeat the installation command.</li>
